.TH "spolicy" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
spolicy \- setup scheduling policy at gRouter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
spolicy show
spolicy set rr | drr
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

Packets wait in the router's queues (see the queue command), one queue per class of traffic. A
single scheduler takes them out and passes them on. When the router is busy, the scheduling
policy decides whose packets go first, which is how one class gets more of a congested link than
another.

.B rr
(round robin, the default) takes one packet from each non-empty queue in turn. It is simple but
ignores the queue weights, and it counts packets, not bytes: a queue of large packets gets more
bandwidth than a queue of small ones.

.B drr
(deficit round robin) gives each queue a share of bytes in proportion to its weight on every
round. A queue sends while its share lasts, charged by each packet's real length, and carries any
unused share to the next round. A congested link is then divided by weight, in bytes, whatever
the packet sizes.

The difference only shows when packets queue up. On an idle router every packet leaves at once
under either policy.

.B show
prints the active policy;
.B set
changes it at once.

.SH EXAMPLES

.RS
.nf
spolicy show
spolicy set drr
.fi
.RE

